---
Title: MetaPost Diagrams
---

# MetaPost diagrams (the `@begin(metapost)` environment)

[MetaPost](https://www.tug.org/metapost.html) is a small, elegant programming
language for producing precise vector graphics. A descendant of Knuth's Metafont,
it excels at exactly the figures a technical or scholarly document wants — geometric
constructions, labelled diagrams, function plots, anything you can describe with
coordinates, paths, and a little arithmetic. JMarkdown's `metapost` environment lets
you write that drawing code directly in your document. When the file is processed,
JMarkdown compiles the diagram with the real MetaPost engine and drops the result
into the page where the environment stood.

Like the [TiKZ](tikz-diagrams.html) directive, MetaPost is compiled at authoring
time rather than shipped to the reader's browser — so the same source serves the
flagship JMarkdown use case of one document producing both a polished web page and a
typeset book. On the **web** the diagram becomes a clean, resolution-independent
**SVG**; in **print** (`--to latex`) it becomes a **PDF** included with
`\includegraphics`. Unlike TiKZ (which emits nothing in LaTeX), MetaPost figures
appear in *both* outputs — see [LaTeX output](#latex-output).

This page covers the environment, the toolchain it drives, the caching that keeps
re-processing cheap, the optional attributes that control sizing and embedding, and
how errors surface. It does **not** teach MetaPost itself — that is a language in its
own right, with an excellent [manual](https://www.tug.org/docs/metapost/mpman.pdf).
Everything inside the environment is MetaPost's; everything around it is JMarkdown's.

## Prerequisites

Like TiKZ and Mermaid-for-print, the `metapost` environment shells out to external
programs, so it works only on a machine that has them installed — both ship with
**TeX Live** and **MacTeX**, so if you have a TeX distribution you almost certainly
have them already:

- **`mpost`**, the MetaPost compiler, used for the web SVG.
- **`mptopdf`**, MetaPost's PDF converter, used for the LaTeX/print PDF.

If a required program is missing, the environment reports an error in place of the
diagram (HTML) or drops it with a build warning (LaTeX) rather than failing the whole
build — see [When compilation fails](#when-compilation-fails). On a machine that has
them, nothing more is needed; you do not run MetaPost yourself.

## The basics

A MetaPost diagram is a [block environment](block-environments.html): open it with
`@begin(metapost)`, write the drawing code as the body, then close it with
`@end(metapost)`. The body is taken **verbatim** — the Markdown lexer never touches
it, so `;`, `:=`, `--`, `*`, `#`, and every other character MetaPost needs survive
intact.

```
@begin(metapost)
draw (0,0)--(100,0)--(50,80)--cycle withpen pencircle scaled 1.5;
fill fullcircle scaled 24 shifted (50,30) withcolor (0.85,0.2,0.2);
@end(metapost)
```

The live demo below shows that same source on the left and the compiled diagram on
the right, so the two can never drift apart:

::::markdown-demo
@begin(metapost){embed=true}
draw (0,0)--(100,0)--(50,80)--cycle withpen pencircle scaled 1.5;
fill fullcircle scaled 24 shifted (50,30) withcolor (0.85,0.2,0.2);
@end(metapost)
::::

Note what you did *not* write. You did not open a `beginfig`, declare an `end`, or
set up a figure — the environment supplies that scaffolding for you (see
[What gets compiled](#what-gets-compiled)); you write only the drawing commands.

### Demoing a diagram needs a wrapper

Every example on this page shows a `metapost` block *inside* a `markdown-demo`
container so you can see source and output together. In your own documents you will
normally write a plain `@begin(metapost) … @end(metapost)` block on its own, with no
surrounding demo.

## What gets compiled

Around your body, JMarkdown assembles a minimal, complete MetaPost program before
handing it to the engine:

- **The body is wrapped in a figure when needed.** If your body does not already
  contain a `beginfig`, JMarkdown wraps it in `beginfig(1); … endfig;` for you —
  which is why the example above could start straight in with `draw`. If you *do*
  write your own `beginfig(n); … endfig;`, that is honoured untouched, so you keep
  full control (and can define several figures — see
  [Multiple figures](#multiple-figures)).
- **A trailing `end.` is added** if the program does not already finish with one, so
  MetaPost always sees a well-formed program.

Because MetaPost is a full language, the body can do real work — loops, arithmetic,
variables, definitions. This programmability is its great strength:

::::markdown-demo
@begin(metapost){embed=true}
numeric n; n = 12;
for i=0 upto n-1:
  draw (0,0)--(40,0) rotated (360i/n) withpen pencircle scaled 1
       withcolor (i/n, 0.3, 1-i/n);
endfor
draw fullcircle scaled 80 dashed evenly;
@end(metapost)
::::

Labels come in two forms, and the difference matters. A **plain string** —
`"origin"` — is set *literally* in MetaPost's default font, so it is right for
ordinary words but shows mathematics verbatim, dollar signs and all. To *typeset*
mathematics (or any TeX), wrap the snippet in `btex … etex`: MetaPost hands it to TeX
during compilation and embeds the typeset result in the figure. Note that `btex … etex`
uses **plain TeX**, so plain-TeX math such as `$P_1$` or `$x^2+y^2$` works out of the
box; LaTeX-only constructs (e.g. `\frac`) would need a `verbatimtex %&latex … etex`
preamble.

::::markdown-demo
@begin(metapost){embed=true}
z0 = (0,0); z1 = (80,0); z2 = (80,50); z3 = (0,50);
draw z0--z1--z2--z3--cycle;
label.bot("origin", z0);               % a plain string — literal text
label.top(btex $P_1$ etex, z2);        % btex … etex — typeset by TeX
dotlabel.rt(btex $x^2 + y^2$ etex, (40,25));
@end(metapost)
::::

## How the diagram becomes an image

When JMarkdown meets a `metapost` block, it runs a small, deterministic pipeline. The
working directory for all of it is a folder named `MetaPost/`, created automatically
**beside the Markdown file being processed** (not the directory you ran the command
from). The steps, for the **web/SVG** path, are:

1. **Assemble the program** described above, prefixed with the two lines that tell
   MetaPost to emit SVG:

   ```
   outputformat := "svg";
   outputtemplate := "%j-%c.svg";
   ```

   and compute an MD5 **hash** of the complete contents. That hash is the diagram's
   identity: change a single character and the hash changes; leave it alone and the
   hash is stable across runs.

2. **Check the cache.** The compiled SVG is stored as `MetaPost/{hash}-1.svg`. If it
   already exists, the diagram is unchanged since last time, so compilation is
   skipped and the file reused. This is what makes re-processing a document with many
   diagrams fast — only diagrams you have edited are recompiled.

3. **Compile, if needed.** On a cache miss, JMarkdown writes `MetaPost/{hash}.mp` and
   runs `mpost` on it in the `MetaPost/` directory.

4. **Clean up.** The intermediate `.mp`, `.log`, and other working files are deleted,
   leaving just the `MetaPost/{hash}-N.svg` output(s) in the cache.

5. **Emit the image.** The environment renders an `<img>` pointing at (or embedding)
   that SVG (see [Sizing and embedding](#sizing-and-embedding)).

The upshot: the `MetaPost/` folder is a build cache. You can safely delete it — the
diagrams will simply recompile on the next run — and you can commit it to keep
diagrams reproducible without requiring every collaborator to have a TeX
installation. To force a clean rebuild of *every* diagram, see the `empty-cache`
attribute below.

## Sizing and embedding

A `metapost` block takes its options as an attribute block, `{…}`, on the opening
line — the same `key="value"` attribute syntax used throughout JMarkdown. Three
control the web output, plus a cache-control attribute. (These mirror the
[TiKZ](tikz-diagrams.html#sizing-and-embedding) attributes exactly.)

### `scale` — visually scale the rendered SVG

`scale` takes a number and scales the *displayed* image with a CSS transform
(`transform: scale(N)`, anchored at the top-left). It does not re-typeset the
diagram; it stretches or shrinks the finished SVG in the browser.

```
@begin(metapost){scale=1.5}
draw fullcircle scaled 60;
@end(metapost)
```

For sharper results at a fixed size, scale *inside* MetaPost instead (multiply your
coordinates, or draw into a `picture` and `scale` it), and reserve the `scale`
attribute for quick visual nudges.

### `width` — set a CSS width

`width` takes any CSS length and is applied as `style="width: …"`. Use a normal unit
such as `6cm`, or a percentage to make the figure responsive to its container:

```
@begin(metapost){width='100%'}
draw (0,0)--(4cm,0)--(2cm,3cm)--cycle;
@end(metapost)
```

`scale` and `width` are mutually exclusive: if you supply both, `scale` takes effect
and `width` is ignored.

### `embed` — inline the SVG as a data URI

By default the `<img>` *links* to the SVG file: `<img src='MetaPost/{hash}-1.svg'>`.
That keeps the HTML small but means the page needs the `MetaPost/` folder alongside it
to display. Set `embed=true` (or simply `embed`) and the environment instead inlines
the SVG as a base64 `data:` URI:

```
@begin(metapost){embed=true}
draw unitsquare scaled 50;
@end(metapost)
```

Use `embed` when you want a single, self-contained HTML file with no external image
dependencies — which is exactly why the side-by-side demos on this page embed. The
trade-off is a larger HTML file, since the image travels inside it.

### `empty-cache` — force a full rebuild

`empty-cache=true` (or bare `empty-cache`) deletes **every** file in the `MetaPost/`
folder before processing, forcing all diagrams in the run to recompile from scratch.
Reach for it when something outside the diagram's own source has changed that the
per-diagram hash cannot detect — a new MetaPost version, a corrupted cache. For
everyday editing the content hash already rebuilds exactly the diagrams you changed,
so you will rarely need it.

## LaTeX output

In the LaTeX pipeline (`--to latex`) the `metapost` environment produces a genuine
figure — this is the key difference from the [TiKZ](tikz-diagrams.html#latex-output)
directive, which emits nothing in LaTeX. JMarkdown converts the same MetaPost source
to a cached **PDF** with `mptopdf` and includes it:

```latex
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[max width=\linewidth]{…/MetaPost/{hash}-1.pdf}
\end{center}
```

The PDF route is deliberately **engine-agnostic**: it works with the default
`pdflatex` (as well as `xelatex` / `lualatex`) with no extra packages beyond
`adjustbox`, which JMarkdown loads for you to provide `max width`. You do **not** need
LuaLaTeX or `luamplib`. The `scale` and `width` attributes are HTML-only; in LaTeX the
figure is sized to `max width=\linewidth`.

The PDFs are cached under `MetaPost/{hash}-N.pdf` on the same content-hash basis as
the SVGs (the LaTeX and web caches are kept separate, so switching output formats
never clobbers the other's files).

## Multiple figures

A single `metapost` block usually contains one figure. If you write your own
`beginfig(n)` blocks, though, each one becomes its own image, emitted in figure order
— so a block with `beginfig(1) … endfig; beginfig(2) … endfig;` produces two images.
The auto-wrapping in [What gets compiled](#what-gets-compiled) only applies when your
body contains *no* `beginfig` at all.

## When compilation fails

If MetaPost exits with an error — a syntax mistake, an undefined variable, or the
tool not being installed — JMarkdown does **not** abort the whole build:

- In **HTML**, in place of the diagram it emits a small error box reading *"Error
  processing MetaPost diagram"* with a **Show log** disclosure; expand it to read the
  captured MetaPost log and fix your source.
- In **LaTeX**, the figure is dropped and a build warning is recorded (it appears in
  the stderr summary at the end of the build, and in [watch mode](watch-mode.html) as
  an amber banner).

Either way, any half-written output from the failed run is swept away — MetaPost can
emit a partial figure *and* still report an error, so cleaning up guarantees the next
run starts fresh rather than caching a broken image.

## Reference

### Environment

| | |
|---|---|
| Syntax | `@begin(metapost)` … MetaPost body … `@end(metapost)` |
| Kind | [Block environment](block-environments.html) (`@begin … @end`) |
| Body processing | Verbatim — the body is **not** run through the Markdown lexer |
| Auto-wrapping | A body with no `beginfig` is wrapped in `beginfig(1); … endfig;`; a trailing `end.` is added if absent |
| HTML output | `<img>` referencing or embedding the compiled SVG |
| LaTeX output | `\includegraphics[max width=\linewidth]` of a `mptopdf`-generated PDF, centred |
| Toolchain | `mpost` (SVG, web) · `mptopdf` (PDF, print) — both from TeX Live / MacTeX |
| Cache | `MetaPost/{md5-hash}-N.{svg,pdf}`, beside the source file; reused on an exact-content hit |

### Attributes

| Attribute | Value | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `scale` | number | Scales the displayed SVG via `transform: scale(N)` (origin top-left). Overrides `width`. HTML only. |
| `width` | CSS length | Sets `style="width: …"` (e.g. `6cm`, `100%`). Ignored if `scale` is also given. HTML only. |
| `embed` | `true` / bare | Inlines the SVG as a base64 `data:` URI for a self-contained page; otherwise the `<img>` links to `MetaPost/{hash}-1.svg`. HTML only. |
| `empty-cache` | `true` / bare | Deletes the entire `MetaPost/` cache before processing, forcing every diagram to recompile. |

